killercoda Linux Fundamentals lesson 2 

Goal:
learn how to use the 'man' command to look up documantation for linux commands- the manual pages built into the system.

What I did:
  - Opended the manuel page for 'man' itself with 'man man' and read through its sections: NAME, SYNOPSIS, and DESCRIPTION.
  - Learned that the manuel is split into numbered sections (1-9): 1 is user commands, 2 system calls, 3 library functions, 4 special files, 5 file formats, 6 games, 7 miscellaneous, 8 system administration commands, 9 kernal routines.
  - looked up a command I already know with 'man ls'.
  - Tried a section-specific lookup with 'man 8 ls' and got no manuelentry for ls in section 8.
  - Used 'man -f ls' to get the one-line description: ls (1) - list directory contents.
  - Used 'man -f intro' to list the intro page for every section, then opended two of the 'man 1 intro' and 'man 8 imtro'.
  - Finished the lesson

  What I got wrong:
  - When I ran 'man man' an error came up: can't resolve man7/groff_man.7.
  - I first looked for 'ls' in section 8 before checking which section it's actually in - it's in section 1.

  What I learned:
    - 'man <command>' is the built-in help system - I don't have to leave the terminal 
  
